  "worked_solution": "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-0.5)$ | $-0.5$ | $(-0.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/608c88c65eff4c63520a7a5a4a51a468601b0d6edcf5f063475c8429e1308962.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
